VALUES (
  '2026/11/21/hdq-month-october-2026',
  'month',
  'month',
  'Oil Was the Shock. Rates and Cash Flow Told the Rest of the Story.',
  'October’s energy shock raised the risk of persistent inflation. The Bank of Canada hiked even as hiring weakened, the Canadian dollar strengthened, and the TSX ended the month with a sharp selloff. The same shock reached Canadian clients through very different channels.',
  '<ul><li><strong>Gasoline widened the inflation split.</strong><span> September CPI rose 3.1% year over year, compared with 2.2% excluding gasoline. The Bank of Canada lifted the overnight rate to 2.50% on October 28.</span></li><li><strong>The hike and the five-year yield went different ways.</strong><span> The five-year Canada benchmark fell 11 basis points from October 1 to 30. It is a reference for funding costs, while each lender sets its own offers.</span></li><li><strong>The Canadian dollar ended October stronger against the US dollar.</strong><span> The Bank of Canada’s indicative rate moved from C$1.4243 per US dollar on October 1 to C$1.3960 on October 30, increasing the currency drag on unhedged US holdings over that span.</span></li><li><strong>Oil and the TSX split on the last trading day.</strong><span> December Brent settled at US$117.64 as the contract expired. The TSX closed down 1.74% as technology and metal miners led the decline.</span></li><li><strong>Year-end financial planning had its own calendar.</strong><span> Federal payroll and Home Buyers’ Plan changes had already received Royal Assent. The October 20 announcement fixed the budget date, while its policy contents were still unknown.</span></li></ul>',
  '<style>.mia-lede{font-size:18px;line-height:1.6;color:#15233b}.mia-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:13px;margin:8px 0 14px}@media(max-width:680px){.mia-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}}.mia-stat{background:#fff;border:1px solid #e4e7ec;border-top:3px solid #1a3560;padding:15px 15px 13px;border-radius:3px}.mia-stat.g{border-top-color:#e8a825}.mia-stat.u{border-top-color:#3a7a55}.mia-stat.d{border-top-color:#8a3030}.mia-stat .v{font-family:"Bricolage Grotesque",sans-serif;font-weight:800;font-size:27px;line-height:1;color:#15233b;letter-spacing:-.02em}.mia-stat .v small{font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#8b93a0}.mia-stat .l{font-size:11px;color:#6b7280;margin-top:8px;line-height:1.35;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;font-weight:600}.mia-stat .c{font-size:12px;font-weight:700;margin-top:6px}.mia-stat .c.p{color:#3a7a55}.mia-stat .c.n{color:#8a3030}.mia-stat .c.f{color:#7e93b4}.mia-pull{border-left:3px solid #e8a825;padding:6px 0 6px 22px;margin:30px 0;font-family:"Bricolage Grotesque",sans-serif;font-weight:600;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32;color:#1a3560}.mia-scen{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:14px;margin:14px 0 6px}@media(max-width:760px){.mia-scen{grid-template-columns:1fr}}.mia-card{border:1px solid #e4e7ec;border-radius:4px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.mia-card .t{padding:13px 15px;color:#fff}.mia-card.base .t{background:#1a3560}.mia-card.bull .t{background:#2c5f44}.mia-card.bear .t{background:#6e2a2a}.mia-card .t .nm{font-family:"Bricolage Grotesque",sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:16px}.mia-card .t .pr{font-size:11px;opacity:.82;margin-top:2px}.mia-card .bd{padding:13px 15px;font-size:13.5px;flex:1}.mia-card .bd p{margin-bottom:9px}.mia-card .tr{border-top:1px solid #e4e7ec;padding:11px 15px;background:#f7f8fa}.mia-card .tr .lb{font-size:9.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6b7280;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:6px}.mia-card .tr ul{list-style:none;font-size:12px;margin:0;padding:0}.mia-card .tr li{padding:3px 0;color:#15233b}.mia-card .tr li:before{content:"\25B8 ";color:#c98e16}.mia-tbl-w{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid #e4e7ec;border-radius:4px;margin:14px 0}.mia-tbl{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:12.5px;min-width:600px}.mia-tbl th,.mia-tbl td{padding:8px 12px;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid #eef1f5;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.mia-tbl th{background:#1a3560;color:#fff;font-weight:600;font-size:11px}.mia-tbl th:first-child,.mia-tbl td:first-child{text-align:left}.mia-tbl td.na{color:#c8cdd5}</style><p class="mia-lede">A barrel of oil could cost more, a Canadian mortgage benchmark could end the month lower, and a Canadian investor could lose more on a US stock than its US quotation suggested. All three happened or could be observed in October’s pricing. The useful question heading into year-end is which measures keep moving together once the immediate oil news changes.</p><p>Editorial cutoff: October 31, 2026 at 23:59:59 Toronto time.</p><h2 id="glance" data-toc="The Month at a Glance" data-toc-n="01"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Section 01</span>The Month at a Glance</h2><div class="mia-grid"><div class="mia-stat u"><div class="v">2.50%</div><div class="l">Overnight rate</div><div class="c p">Oct 28 · +25 bp</div></div><div class="mia-stat u"><div class="v">3.1%</div><div class="l">September CPI</div><div class="c p">Year over year</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v">2.2%</div><div class="l">CPI excluding gasoline</div><div class="c n">Year over year</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v">−68,000</div><div class="l">September employment</div><div class="c n">Approximate monthly change</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v">3.51%</div><div class="l">Canada five-year yield</div><div class="c n">Oct 30 · −11 bp since Oct 1</div></div><div class="mia-stat "><div class="v">1.3960</div><div class="l">CAD per US dollar</div><div class="c f">Oct 30 · indicative average</div></div><div class="mia-stat u"><div class="v">US$117.64</div><div class="l">December Brent</div><div class="c p">Oct 30 · expiry settlement</div></div><div class="mia-stat d"><div class="v">35,620.03</div><div class="l">S&amp;P/TSX Composite</div><div class="c n">Oct 30 · −1.74% on the day</div></div></div><p>The dashboard pairs September economic measures released in October with prices from the final trading day. Each label identifies its measurement window.</p><h2 id="arc" data-toc="The Arc of the Month" data-toc-n="02"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Section 02</span>The Arc of the Month</h2><p>Early October’s HDQ coverage started with the tension between bond pricing and weak growth. On October 9 the September Labour Force Survey gave the growth side a firmer footing. The October 19 CPI and consumer surveys clarified the inflation side. On October 28 the Bank of Canada raised rates and the Fed held. Two days later Statistics Canada’s GDP flash reading and a sharp equity selloff gave a different view of the risks that remained. Five relationships from across the desks explain why a single oil, rate or index forecast would leave important exposures untested.</p><h2 id="p1" data-toc="From the Strait of Hormuz to the gas pump" data-toc-n="P1" data-toc-sub="1"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Pattern 1</span>From the Strait of Hormuz to the gas pump</h2><p>The oil risk showed up at different points in the supply chain. The Geopolitical desk documented the gap between crude available for immediate delivery and futures for later delivery, while the Economy and Behavioural desks followed what Canadians actually noticed at the pump. The October 30 settlement made the distinction tangible. Reuters reported US$117.64 on the expiring December Brent contract and US$113.43 for the more active January contract. It also reported a tanker attack in the strait and a WTI settlement of US$104.12. A futures quote can price disruption without measuring the physical volumes still missing.</p><p>September gasoline prices were up 27.8% year over year. The CPI excluding gasoline slowed to 2.2% from 2.4% in August. The Bank’s October 19 survey, fielded mainly in August, found longer-term expectations had risen to meet already elevated shorter-term expectations. Trade uncertainty was another factor in that survey. October’s prices cannot explain responses collected in August, but the earlier results show the channel the Bank was concerned about on October 28. A visible price shock can change expectations before the broader basket confirms a change.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#FFFFFF;border:1px solid #D0D0D0;width:100%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#F9F9F9;border-bottom:1px solid #ECECEC;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;column-gap:16px;row-gap:5px;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:12px"><span style="color:#1A3560;font-weight:700">The gasoline inflation gap</span><span style="color:#4A5568;font-weight:600">All-items CPI minus CPI excluding gasoline</span><span style="color:#56585B;font-weight:400">October vintage through September</span><span style="color:#56585B;font-weight:400">21 observations</span></div><div><script>(() => {
  const data = {"kind":"area","labels":["2025-01","2025-02","2025-03","2025-04","2025-05","2025-06","2025-07","2025-08","2025-09","2025-10","2025-11","2025-12","2026-01","2026-02","2026-03","2026-04","2026-05","2026-06","2026-07","2026-08","2026-09"],"marks":[[0,"Jan 25"],[5,"Jun 25"],[9,"Oct 25"],[14,"Mar 26"],[20,"Sep 26"]],"domain":[-1.2,1.2],"ticks":[-1,-0.5,0,0.5,1],"places":1,"series":[{"values":[0.2,0.0,-0.2,-0.9,-0.8,-0.6,-0.8,-0.5,-0.2,-0.4,-0.4,-0.6,-0.7,-0.6,0.2,0.8,1.0,0.6,0.8,0.6,0.9],"color":"ink"}],"suffix":"","signed":true,"title":"The gasoline inflation gap","unit":"Gap in percentage points","locale":"en-CA"};
  const script = document.currentScript;
  const svg = document.createElementNS(''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'', ''svg'');
  svg.setAttribute(''viewBox'', ''0 0 680 340'');
  svg.setAttribute(''width'', ''100%'');
  svg.setAttribute(''role'', ''img'');
  svg.setAttribute(''aria-label'', data.title);
  svg.style.display = ''block'';
  script.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
  const palette = {
    green:''#397955'', red:''#8A3030'', paleGreen:''#F0F6F0'', paleRed:''#FCF5F4'',
    gold:''#E8A825'', ink:''#4A5568'', grey:''#56585B'', muted:''#8B99AF'',
    navy:''#1A3560'', white:''#FFFFFF'', outer:''#F9F9F9'', grid:''#D0D0D0'', divider:''#ECECEC''
  };
  function add(tag, attrs, label) {
    const node = document.createElementNS(''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'',tag);
    Object.entries(attrs).forEach(([key,value]) => node.setAttribute(key,value));
    if (label !== undefined) node.textContent = label;
    svg.appendChild(node);
    return node;
  }
  const L=74, R=584, T=49, B=267;
  const x = i => L + i/(data.labels.length-1)*(R-L);
  const y = v => B - (v-data.domain[0])/(data.domain[1]-data.domain[0])*(B-T);
  const label = (left, top, message, attrs={}) => add(''text'',{
    x:left,y:top,fill:palette.ink,''font-family'':''Arial, sans-serif'',''font-size'':14,...attrs
  },message);
  const line = (x1,y1,x2,y2,stroke,attrs={}) => add(''line'',{x1,y1,x2,y2,stroke,...attrs});
  const number = (v, places=data.places) => new Intl.NumberFormat(data.locale,{
    minimumFractionDigits:places,maximumFractionDigits:places
  }).format(v);
  add(''rect'',{x:0,y:0,width:680,height:340,fill:palette.white});
  label(L,26,data.unit,{''font-weight'':700});
  if (data.kind===''area'') {
    add(''rect'',{x:L,y:T,width:R-L,height:y(0)-T,fill:palette.paleRed});
    add(''rect'',{x:L,y:y(0),width:R-L,height:B-y(0),fill:palette.paleGreen});
  }
  data.ticks.forEach(v => {
    line(L,y(v),R,y(v),v===0?palette.muted:palette.grid,{''stroke-width'':v===0?1:0.65});
    label(L-10,y(v)+5,number(v),{''text-anchor'':''end'',fill:palette.grey});
  });
  data.marks.forEach(([i, name]) => {
    line(x(i),B,x(i),B+5,palette.muted,{''stroke-width'':0.75});
    label(x(i),B+25,name,{''text-anchor'':''middle'',fill:palette.grey});
  });
  if (data.event) {
    const [i,name] = data.event;
    line(x(i),T,x(i),B,palette.muted,{''stroke-width'':1,''stroke-dasharray'':''4 5''});
    label(x(i)-6,T+16,name,{''text-anchor'':''end'',fill:palette.grey,''font-size'':12,
      stroke:palette.white,''stroke-width'':4,''paint-order'':''stroke''});
  }
  data.series.forEach(({values,color,dashed}) => {
    if (data.kind===''area'') {
      values.slice(1).forEach((v,j) => {
        const a=values[j], pos=v>=0;
        const fill = pos => pos ? palette.red : palette.green;
        const shade = (x1,v1,x2,v2,ink) => add(''path'',{
          d:''M ''+x1+'' ''+y(0)+'' L ''+x1+'' ''+y(v1)+'' L ''+x2+'' ''+y(v2)+'' L ''+x2+'' ''+y(0)+'' Z'',
          fill:ink,''fill-opacity'':0.16
        });
        if ((a<0 && v>0)||(a>0 && v<0)) {
          const cross=x(j)+(x(j+1)-x(j))*Math.abs(a)/(Math.abs(a)+Math.abs(v));
          shade(x(j),a,cross,0,fill(a>=0));
          shade(cross,0,x(j+1),v,fill(pos));
        } else {
          shade(x(j),a,x(j+1),v,fill(a+v>=0));
        }
      });
    }
    add(''polyline'',{
      points:values.map((v,i)=>x(i)+'',''+y(v)).join('' ''),
      fill:''none'',stroke:palette[color],''stroke-width'':dashed?1.45:1.8,
      ''stroke-dasharray'':dashed?''6 4'':''none'',''stroke-linejoin'':''round'',''stroke-linecap'':''round''
    });
  });
  if (data.legend) {
    data.legend.forEach(([name,col,dash],i)=>{
      const left=L+i*239;
      line(left,318,left+28,318,palette[col],{''stroke-width'':1.7,''stroke-dasharray'':dash?''6 4'':''none''});
      label(left+35,322,name,{fill:palette.grey,''font-size'':13});
    });
  }
  const finish=data.series[0].values.at(-1);
  const value=(data.signed && finish>0?''+'':'''')+number(finish)+data.suffix;
  const py=y(finish);
  const width=Math.max(49,value.length*8+12);
  add(''circle'',{cx:R,cy:py,r:3.1,fill:palette[data.series[0].color]});
  add(''rect'',{x:R+8,y:py-8,width,height:16,rx:7,fill:palette.gold});
  label(R+8+width/2,py+4.4,value,{''text-anchor'':''middle'',''font-size'':12,''font-weight'':700,fill:palette.navy});
})();
</script></div><div style="font-size:11px;padding:6px 14px 10px;color:#56585B">Source · <a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/261019/cg-a001-eng.htm">Statistics Canada CPI chart 1, Oct 19</a> &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div></div><p class="chart-caption">The gap between the two 12-month rates widened from +0.6 points in August to +0.9 in September. This is a difference between two indexes and does not measure gasoline’s weighted contribution.</p></div><h2 id="p2" data-toc="Policy rates and borrowing costs parted ways" data-toc-n="P2" data-toc-sub="1"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Pattern 2</span>Policy rates and borrowing costs parted ways</h2><p>The Economy desk’s early-month question was whether financial conditions had already tightened before a decision. By October 28 the Bank explicitly acted to contain the spread of higher energy costs. The Fed held its range at 3.75% to 4.00%, with two voters favouring an increase. The difference between the Fed range midpoint and the Canadian overnight target narrowed from 162.5 to 137.5 basis points. The Canadian five-year benchmark still ended below its first October reading, and the US 10-year Treasury yield fell from 5.24% to 4.94% over the same dates.</p><p>On October 9 Statistics Canada reported about 68,000 fewer jobs for September and 6.5% unemployment. On October 30 it reported no August GDP growth and a preliminary 0.1% September decline. It also estimated 0.3% third-quarter growth by industry on a quarterly basis. The Bank’s earlier October report put third-quarter growth around 2% annualized. The quarterly pace and annualized rate use different units, and the industry flash and the Bank’s estimate are different measures. The weaker reading challenges the pace assumed in the Bank’s assessment while leaving the official quarterly release as the next test.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#FFFFFF;border:1px solid #D0D0D0;width:100%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#F9F9F9;border-bottom:1px solid #ECECEC;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;column-gap:16px;row-gap:5px;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:12px"><span style="color:#1A3560;font-weight:700">The policy rate rose and benchmark yields fell</span><span style="color:#4A5568;font-weight:600">Canada 5-year and US 10-year</span><span style="color:#56585B;font-weight:400">Matched October sessions</span><span style="color:#56585B;font-weight:400">21 observations</span></div><div><script>(() => {
  const data = {"kind":"line","labels":["1","2","5","6","7","8","9","13","14","15","16","19","20","21","22","23","26","27","28","29","30"],"marks":[[0,"Oct 1"],[6,"Oct 9"],[10,"Oct 16"],[15,"Oct 23"],[20,"Oct 30"]],"domain":[-35,15],"ticks":[-30,-20,-10,0,10],"places":0,"series":[{"values":[0,-2,1,-3,-2,-2,-8,-7,-7,-9,-8,2,0,0,-12,-6,-4,-5,0,-4,-11],"color":"navy"},{"values":[0,4,7,3,4,-2,0,-3,-5,-14,-15,-9,-12,-11,-28,-24,-24,-25,-19,-26,-30],"color":"ink","dashed":true}],"suffix":"","event":[18,"Rate decisions"],"legend":[["Canada 5-year","navy",false],["US 10-year","ink",true]],"title":"The policy rate rose and benchmark yields fell","unit":"Change since October 1, bp","locale":"en-CA"};
  const script = document.currentScript;
  const svg = document.createElementNS(''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'', ''svg'');
  svg.setAttribute(''viewBox'', ''0 0 680 340'');
  svg.setAttribute(''width'', ''100%'');
  svg.setAttribute(''role'', ''img'');
  svg.setAttribute(''aria-label'', data.title);
  svg.style.display = ''block'';
  script.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
  const palette = {
    green:''#397955'', red:''#8A3030'', paleGreen:''#F0F6F0'', paleRed:''#FCF5F4'',
    gold:''#E8A825'', ink:''#4A5568'', grey:''#56585B'', muted:''#8B99AF'',
    navy:''#1A3560'', white:''#FFFFFF'', outer:''#F9F9F9'', grid:''#D0D0D0'', divider:''#ECECEC''
  };
  function add(tag, attrs, label) {
    const node = document.createElementNS(''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'',tag);
    Object.entries(attrs).forEach(([key,value]) => node.setAttribute(key,value));
    if (label !== undefined) node.textContent = label;
    svg.appendChild(node);
    return node;
  }
  const L=74, R=584, T=49, B=267;
  const x = i => L + i/(data.labels.length-1)*(R-L);
  const y = v => B - (v-data.domain[0])/(data.domain[1]-data.domain[0])*(B-T);
  const label = (left, top, message, attrs={}) => add(''text'',{
    x:left,y:top,fill:palette.ink,''font-family'':''Arial, sans-serif'',''font-size'':14,...attrs
  },message);
  const line = (x1,y1,x2,y2,stroke,attrs={}) => add(''line'',{x1,y1,x2,y2,stroke,...attrs});
  const number = (v, places=data.places) => new Intl.NumberFormat(data.locale,{
    minimumFractionDigits:places,maximumFractionDigits:places
  }).format(v);
  add(''rect'',{x:0,y:0,width:680,height:340,fill:palette.white});
  label(L,26,data.unit,{''font-weight'':700});
  if (data.kind===''area'') {
    add(''rect'',{x:L,y:T,width:R-L,height:y(0)-T,fill:palette.paleRed});
    add(''rect'',{x:L,y:y(0),width:R-L,height:B-y(0),fill:palette.paleGreen});
  }
  data.ticks.forEach(v => {
    line(L,y(v),R,y(v),v===0?palette.muted:palette.grid,{''stroke-width'':v===0?1:0.65});
    label(L-10,y(v)+5,number(v),{''text-anchor'':''end'',fill:palette.grey});
  });
  data.marks.forEach(([i, name]) => {
    line(x(i),B,x(i),B+5,palette.muted,{''stroke-width'':0.75});
    label(x(i),B+25,name,{''text-anchor'':''middle'',fill:palette.grey});
  });
  if (data.event) {
    const [i,name] = data.event;
    line(x(i),T,x(i),B,palette.muted,{''stroke-width'':1,''stroke-dasharray'':''4 5''});
    label(x(i)-6,T+16,name,{''text-anchor'':''end'',fill:palette.grey,''font-size'':12,
      stroke:palette.white,''stroke-width'':4,''paint-order'':''stroke''});
  }
  data.series.forEach(({values,color,dashed}) => {
    if (data.kind===''area'') {
      values.slice(1).forEach((v,j) => {
        const a=values[j], pos=v>=0;
        const fill = pos => pos ? palette.red : palette.green;
        const shade = (x1,v1,x2,v2,ink) => add(''path'',{
          d:''M ''+x1+'' ''+y(0)+'' L ''+x1+'' ''+y(v1)+'' L ''+x2+'' ''+y(v2)+'' L ''+x2+'' ''+y(0)+'' Z'',
          fill:ink,''fill-opacity'':0.16
        });
        if ((a<0 && v>0)||(a>0 && v<0)) {
          const cross=x(j)+(x(j+1)-x(j))*Math.abs(a)/(Math.abs(a)+Math.abs(v));
          shade(x(j),a,cross,0,fill(a>=0));
          shade(cross,0,x(j+1),v,fill(pos));
        } else {
          shade(x(j),a,x(j+1),v,fill(a+v>=0));
        }
      });
    }
    add(''polyline'',{
      points:values.map((v,i)=>x(i)+'',''+y(v)).join('' ''),
      fill:''none'',stroke:palette[color],''stroke-width'':dashed?1.45:1.8,
      ''stroke-dasharray'':dashed?''6 4'':''none'',''stroke-linejoin'':''round'',''stroke-linecap'':''round''
    });
  });
  if (data.legend) {
    data.legend.forEach(([name,col,dash],i)=>{
      const left=L+i*239;
      line(left,318,left+28,318,palette[col],{''stroke-width'':1.7,''stroke-dasharray'':dash?''6 4'':''none''});
      label(left+35,322,name,{fill:palette.grey,''font-size'':13});
    });
  }
  const finish=data.series[0].values.at(-1);
  const value=(data.signed && finish>0?''+'':'''')+number(finish)+data.suffix;
  const py=y(finish);
  const width=Math.max(49,value.length*8+12);
  add(''circle'',{cx:R,cy:py,r:3.1,fill:palette[data.series[0].color]});
  add(''rect'',{x:R+8,y:py-8,width,height:16,rx:7,fill:palette.gold});
  label(R+8+width/2,py+4.4,value,{''text-anchor'':''middle'',''font-size'':12,''font-weight'':700,fill:palette.navy});
})();
</script></div><div style="font-size:11px;padding:6px 14px 10px;color:#56585B">Sources · <a href="https://www.bankofcanada.ca/rates/interest-rates/lookup-bond-yields/?dF=2026-10-01&amp;dT=2026-10-30&amp;rangeType=dates&amp;se=L_V39053">Bank of Canada five-year Canada yield, V39053</a>, <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?field_tdr_date_value=202610&amp;type=daily_treasury_yield_curve">US Treasury daily par yield curve, October</a> &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div></div><p class="chart-caption">Across 21 common sessions, the five-year Canada yield fell 11 basis points and the US 10-year fell 30. The vertical line marks the two central-bank decisions on October 28.</p></div><h2 id="p3" data-toc="A stronger loonie changed the equity result" data-toc-n="P3" data-toc-sub="1"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Pattern 3</span>A stronger loonie changed the equity result</h2><p>The Daily Thread revisited the oil–currency link several times. In the first half of October the Bank of Canada’s indicative USD/CAD rate reached 1.4271 on October 9, the day of the jobs release. By October 30 it was 1.3960. Using the reciprocal, the loonie appreciated about 2.0% from October 1 to 30. A narrowing policy gap and crude strength both fit the direction of the recovery, while weaker domestic activity still provided a plausible counterweight.</p><p>For a Canadian investor, multiply the US-dollar asset value by the CAD cost of one US dollar before comparing start and end values. In this interval the FX multiplier was 1.3960 ÷ 1.4243, or about 0.9801. A flat US asset would have been down about 1.99% in CAD at those rates before fees and hedging. Because the Bank’s series is a daily indicative average, the calculation illustrates translation and does not claim to reproduce an individual fund’s close-to-close performance.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#FFFFFF;border:1px solid #D0D0D0;width:100%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#F9F9F9;border-bottom:1px solid #ECECEC;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;column-gap:16px;row-gap:5px;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:12px"><span style="color:#1A3560;font-weight:700">The loonie rebounded after October 9</span><span style="color:#4A5568;font-weight:600">USD/CAD · daily indicative rate</span><span style="color:#56585B;font-weight:400">Indicative October averages</span><span style="color:#56585B;font-weight:400">21 observations</span></div><div><script>(() => {
  const data = {"kind":"line","labels":["1","2","5","6","7","8","9","13","14","15","16","19","20","21","22","23","26","27","28","29","30"],"marks":[[0,"Oct 1"],[6,"Oct 9"],[10,"Oct 16"],[15,"Oct 23"],[20,"Oct 30"]],"domain":[1.39,1.435],"ticks":[1.39,1.4,1.41,1.42,1.43],"places":4,"series":[{"values":[1.4243,1.4246,1.4254,1.4226,1.4257,1.424,1.4271,1.4222,1.4263,1.4196,1.4154,1.4097,1.4054,1.4057,1.4021,1.3993,1.4025,1.4038,1.4036,1.3985,1.396],"color":"navy"}],"suffix":"","event":[6,"Jobs release"],"title":"The loonie rebounded after October 9","unit":"Canadian dollars per US dollar","locale":"en-CA"};
  const script = document.currentScript;
  const svg = document.createElementNS(''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'', ''svg'');
  svg.setAttribute(''viewBox'', ''0 0 680 340'');
  svg.setAttribute(''width'', ''100%'');
  svg.setAttribute(''role'', ''img'');
  svg.setAttribute(''aria-label'', data.title);
  svg.style.display = ''block'';
  script.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
  const palette = {
    green:''#397955'', red:''#8A3030'', paleGreen:''#F0F6F0'', paleRed:''#FCF5F4'',
    gold:''#E8A825'', ink:''#4A5568'', grey:''#56585B'', muted:''#8B99AF'',
    navy:''#1A3560'', white:''#FFFFFF'', outer:''#F9F9F9'', grid:''#D0D0D0'', divider:''#ECECEC''
  };
  function add(tag, attrs, label) {
    const node = document.createElementNS(''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'',tag);
    Object.entries(attrs).forEach(([key,value]) => node.setAttribute(key,value));
    if (label !== undefined) node.textContent = label;
    svg.appendChild(node);
    return node;
  }
  const L=74, R=584, T=49, B=267;
  const x = i => L + i/(data.labels.length-1)*(R-L);
  const y = v => B - (v-data.domain[0])/(data.domain[1]-data.domain[0])*(B-T);
  const label = (left, top, message, attrs={}) => add(''text'',{
    x:left,y:top,fill:palette.ink,''font-family'':''Arial, sans-serif'',''font-size'':14,...attrs
  },message);
  const line = (x1,y1,x2,y2,stroke,attrs={}) => add(''line'',{x1,y1,x2,y2,stroke,...attrs});
  const number = (v, places=data.places) => new Intl.NumberFormat(data.locale,{
    minimumFractionDigits:places,maximumFractionDigits:places
  }).format(v);
  add(''rect'',{x:0,y:0,width:680,height:340,fill:palette.white});
  label(L,26,data.unit,{''font-weight'':700});
  if (data.kind===''area'') {
    add(''rect'',{x:L,y:T,width:R-L,height:y(0)-T,fill:palette.paleRed});
    add(''rect'',{x:L,y:y(0),width:R-L,height:B-y(0),fill:palette.paleGreen});
  }
  data.ticks.forEach(v => {
    line(L,y(v),R,y(v),v===0?palette.muted:palette.grid,{''stroke-width'':v===0?1:0.65});
    label(L-10,y(v)+5,number(v),{''text-anchor'':''end'',fill:palette.grey});
  });
  data.marks.forEach(([i, name]) => {
    line(x(i),B,x(i),B+5,palette.muted,{''stroke-width'':0.75});
    label(x(i),B+25,name,{''text-anchor'':''middle'',fill:palette.grey});
  });
  if (data.event) {
    const [i,name] = data.event;
    line(x(i),T,x(i),B,palette.muted,{''stroke-width'':1,''stroke-dasharray'':''4 5''});
    label(x(i)-6,T+16,name,{''text-anchor'':''end'',fill:palette.grey,''font-size'':12,
      stroke:palette.white,''stroke-width'':4,''paint-order'':''stroke''});
  }
  data.series.forEach(({values,color,dashed}) => {
    if (data.kind===''area'') {
      values.slice(1).forEach((v,j) => {
        const a=values[j], pos=v>=0;
        const fill = pos => pos ? palette.red : palette.green;
        const shade = (x1,v1,x2,v2,ink) => add(''path'',{
          d:''M ''+x1+'' ''+y(0)+'' L ''+x1+'' ''+y(v1)+'' L ''+x2+'' ''+y(v2)+'' L ''+x2+'' ''+y(0)+'' Z'',
          fill:ink,''fill-opacity'':0.16
        });
        if ((a<0 && v>0)||(a>0 && v<0)) {
          const cross=x(j)+(x(j+1)-x(j))*Math.abs(a)/(Math.abs(a)+Math.abs(v));
          shade(x(j),a,cross,0,fill(a>=0));
          shade(cross,0,x(j+1),v,fill(pos));
        } else {
          shade(x(j),a,x(j+1),v,fill(a+v>=0));
        }
      });
    }
    add(''polyline'',{
      points:values.map((v,i)=>x(i)+'',''+y(v)).join('' ''),
      fill:''none'',stroke:palette[color],''stroke-width'':dashed?1.45:1.8,
      ''stroke-dasharray'':dashed?''6 4'':''none'',''stroke-linejoin'':''round'',''stroke-linecap'':''round''
    });
  });
  if (data.legend) {
    data.legend.forEach(([name,col,dash],i)=>{
      const left=L+i*239;
      line(left,318,left+28,318,palette[col],{''stroke-width'':1.7,''stroke-dasharray'':dash?''6 4'':''none''});
      label(left+35,322,name,{fill:palette.grey,''font-size'':13});
    });
  }
  const finish=data.series[0].values.at(-1);
  const value=(data.signed && finish>0?''+'':'''')+number(finish)+data.suffix;
  const py=y(finish);
  const width=Math.max(49,value.length*8+12);
  add(''circle'',{cx:R,cy:py,r:3.1,fill:palette[data.series[0].color]});
  add(''rect'',{x:R+8,y:py-8,width,height:16,rx:7,fill:palette.gold});
  label(R+8+width/2,py+4.4,value,{''text-anchor'':''middle'',''font-size'':12,''font-weight'':700,fill:palette.navy});
})();
</script></div><div style="font-size:11px;padding:6px 14px 10px;color:#56585B">Source · <a href="https://www.bankofcanada.ca/rates/exchange/daily-exchange-rates-lookup/?dF=2026-10-01&amp;dT=2026-10-30&amp;rangeType=dates&amp;se=FXUSDCAD">Bank of Canada USD/CAD, FXUSDCAD</a> &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div></div><p class="chart-caption">A lower quote means one US dollar buys fewer Canadian dollars. October 9 marks the release of the Labour Force Survey.</p></div><h2 id="p4" data-toc="Rising crude hid exposures inside the index and the trade balance" data-toc-n="P4" data-toc-sub="1"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Pattern 4</span>Rising crude hid exposures inside the index and the trade balance</h2><p>On October 30 the TSX fell 629 points to 35,620.03. Reuters identified weakness in gold miners and technology alongside concern over returns from AI investment. Oil settled higher on the same day. The month’s Market and Weekend coverage showed how different stocks could sit on opposite sides of the same oil shock. Producer revenue can benefit from price increases while other sectors face higher inputs or a tougher discount rate. Gold miners also carry equity and operating exposure, so their behaviour can diverge from bullion.</p><p>The October 6 trade release showed why the same distinction matters outside the market. August energy exports rose 4.7% in value and crude export values rose mainly on prices. Exports excluding energy also rose 1.8%, so there was some breadth in the release. The October 30 GDP report put August oil and gas extraction down 1.2% amid oil sands maintenance. Export receipts and real production answer different questions. A stronger trade headline gives little assurance about the next order book of a tariff-exposed business.</p><h2 id="p5" data-toc="Year-end planning separated enacted rules from the budget" data-toc-n="P5" data-toc-sub="1"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Pattern 5</span>Year-end planning separated enacted rules from the budget</h2><p>The Tax &amp; Wealth desk repeatedly contrasted the 3% prescribed rate with market yields and then focused on enacted payroll and housing provisions. Finance Canada confirmed that Bill C-30 received Royal Assent in June. The combined base Canada Pension Plan contribution rate is set to fall from 9.9% to 9.5% in 2027. The legislation also extends the temporary five-year Home Buyers’ Plan repayment grace period to eligible withdrawals through 2028. The November 5 budget was scheduled as of October 20. Households entered that date with several decisions already governed by existing rules.</p><p>That planning calendar matters more when the same family is watching employment and a mortgage renewal. An expected payroll saving is a modest future cash-flow change. A large RRSP withdrawal for a home, a fixed-term deposit, or the sale of an asset to realize a tax loss can change access to cash now. In each case the practical question is which deadlines and commitments change what the household can afford if income or financing moves.</p><h2 id="forward" data-toc="What the Patterns Imply" data-toc-n="03"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Section 03</span>What the Patterns Imply</h2><p>All horizons begin October 31. The base case assumes an uneven supply adjustment, a detectable but contained spillover from energy into other prices, and soft enough domestic activity to keep policy conditional. Falling nearby oil premiums with softer non-energy prices would shift the view toward faster relief. Persistently high shipping risk alongside broader price increases would shift it the other way.</p><h3>From the Strait of Hormuz to the gas pump</h3><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Fuel budgets may reset faster than confidence</strong> <span>(30-day horizon)</span> Medium. Expensive prompt barrels can keep fuel costs elevated even if later futures soften. A sustained decline in local pump prices would weaken the pressure. Watch retail gasoline and the nearby Brent spread together. Compare actual fuel and heating bills with the household’s plan before changing its spending assumption.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>A narrow inflation shock could become a freight and services problem</strong> <span>(60-day horizon)</span> Medium. Prolonged fuel pressure can move into delivery charges and service contracts as they reprice. Spare capacity can force businesses to absorb costs in margins. Watch CPI excluding gasoline, freight surcharges and firms’ selling-price plans. Stress-test margins separately from sales for business-owner clients.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Longer-term expectations may be slow to improve</strong> <span>(90-day horizon)</span> Medium. Repeated visible price increases can make households plan around a higher inflation rate and demand more cash at hand. Improved trade confidence or a sustained fall in fuel prices could reverse that. Compare short- and long-horizon readings in the next consumer expectations survey, and review what portion of a client’s cash reserve covers actual recurring expenses.</p><h3>Policy rates and borrowing costs parted ways</h3><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Quoted mortgage rates can ease even after a hike</strong> <span>(30-day horizon)</span> Medium. A lower five-year benchmark can offset some pressure on fixed offers, while floating-rate loans can react to prime. Wider funding spreads could prevent lenders from passing the change through. Watch the benchmark and comparable lender quotes. Put the payment dates and rate terms of actual offers side by side.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>A weaker growth confirmation would change the balance of policy risks</strong> <span>(60-day horizon)</span> Medium. If the GDP flash weakness spreads into hours and private demand, there is less scope to pass fuel costs on and less case for repeated hikes. Broad service inflation could outweigh that evidence. Watch the official quarterly release, hours worked and core CPI. Model one further hike and a hold for floating-rate borrowers.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Weak demand can lower government yields while private credit stays expensive</strong> <span>(90-day horizon)</span> Medium. Slower sales and a higher policy rate can cut interest coverage, widening lender and corporate spreads even if government yields fall. A durable rebound in orders would make this less likely. Watch lending standards and the spread in actual refinancing proposals. Run the sensitivity using a higher borrower spread, not only a higher government yield.</p><h3>A stronger loonie changed the equity result</h3><p class="mia-insight"><strong>A stronger CAD can obscure the source of an equity loss</strong> <span>(30-day horizon)</span> High for the arithmetic, medium for persistence. A sustained narrowing of expected rate differences can reinforce the translation headwind. Stronger US data or a more hawkish Fed could reverse it. Track USD and CAD attribution separately in statements and inspect the actual hedge ratio.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>An oil reversal could lower fuel costs and lift the CAD value of US holdings</strong> <span>(60-day horizon)</span> Low to medium. A lower oil premium could reduce expected Canadian tightening and weaken the loonie, lifting the CAD translation of a given US asset value. A broader US dollar decline could overwhelm that channel. Watch crude, matched two-year yields and USD/CAD together. Test both currency paths against real USD expenses.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Currency mismatches can matter most near withdrawal dates</strong> <span>(90-day horizon)</span> High for the mechanism. Liquidating US assets to meet a fixed CAD liability crystallizes the prevailing exchange rate. Matching USD assets to known USD outlays reduces the mismatch, and a weaker CAD can help unhedged positions. Review actual withdrawal dates and test two exchange-rate paths before deciding how much liquidity is needed in each currency.</p><h3>Rising crude hid exposures inside the index and the trade balance</h3><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Rising oil may still leave the index under pressure</strong> <span>(30-day horizon)</span> Medium. If oil sustains inflation concern while miners and tech reprice, gains in energy may fail to outweigh losses elsewhere. A broad advance across banks, miners and industrials would weaken that view. Monitor sector contributions and the share of TSX names advancing rather than the index close alone.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Gold miners and bullion can diverge when input costs rise</strong> <span>(60-day horizon)</span> Medium. Higher diesel costs and tighter risk budgets can offset a stronger gold price for producers. Improvement in realized margins or a new haven bid can close the gap. Compare bullion, miner margins and energy costs. Check what a sleeve intended as a hedge actually owns.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Export receipts can overstate the recovery available to a tariff-hit firm</strong> <span>(90-day horizon)</span> Medium. Higher energy prices or shipments brought forward can lift headline exports while exposed firms face smaller volumes and longer receivable cycles. Broad gains in non-energy volumes and orders would challenge the thesis. Track product-level trade and client backlogs. Stress-test cash conversion at unchanged selling prices.</p><h3>Year-end planning separated enacted rules from the budget</h3><p class="mia-insight"><strong>The budget and household deadlines call for different decisions</strong> <span>(30-day horizon)</span> High for known dates, contingent on any new legislation. A proposal can move sentiment immediately, while its effective date and legal status determine cash flow. Watch the budget text and implementing legislation separately. Sort client actions by deadlines under existing rules and actions that require a proposal to become law.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Tax-loss candidates need a settlement and liquidity check</strong> <span>(60-day horizon)</span> High for the planning mechanism. October’s sector dispersion may leave losses in selected holdings even if the index recovered from prior lows. An early rebound or insufficient usable gains could reduce the benefit. Track adjusted cost base, applicable settlement dates and repurchases that could trigger superficial-loss rules.</p><p class="mia-insight"><strong>Payroll relief and a longer home-plan grace period buy time on different scales</strong> <span>(90-day horizon)</span> High for the enacted measures, medium for household impact. A lower base CPP deduction can modestly increase take-home cash, while delaying eligible HBP repayments can ease early cash pressure but does not rebuild retirement assets. Verify January pay stubs and withdrawal eligibility, then model the later repayment with a higher mortgage cost or temporary income loss.</p><div class="mia-scen"><div class="mia-card base"><div class="t"><div class="nm">Base case</div><div class="pr">Uneven adjustment</div></div><div class="bd"><p>Energy stays expensive enough to keep the Bank attentive, with weak domestic activity restraining pass-through. The yield curve and policy rate can move differently while the TSX’s sector leadership rotates.</p></div><div class="tr"><div class="lb">Watchpoint</div><ul><li>Watch the next non-gas CPI readings, hours worked and actual five-year mortgage quotes.</li></ul></div></div><div class="mia-card bull"><div class="t"><div class="nm">Faster relief</div><div class="pr">Supply normalizes</div></div><div class="bd"><p>Physical availability improves and nearby oil tightness eases. Softer non-energy inflation gives the Bank more room to respond to weak demand, while banks and industrials benefit only if credit quality and orders hold.</p></div><div class="tr"><div class="lb">Watchpoint</div><ul><li>Watch nearby Brent spreads, tanker movements on comparable definitions and broad TSX participation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="mia-card bear"><div class="t"><div class="nm">Adverse case</div><div class="pr">Price pressure spreads</div></div><div class="bd"><p>Shipping disruption persists, fuel and freight costs move into other prices, and weak growth leaves rate-sensitive borrowers and exporters squeezed on both revenue and funding.</p></div><div class="tr"><div class="lb">Watchpoint</div><ul><li>Watch service prices, business pricing intentions and credit spreads alongside freight and crude.</li></ul></div></div></div><h2 id="appendix" data-toc="Data Appendix" data-toc-n="04"><span class="ix" style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#c98e16;letter-spacing:.1em;display:block;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;">Section 04</span>Data Appendix</h2><h3>Daily October observations</h3><div class="mia-tbl-w"><table class="mia-tbl"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Canada 5-year (%)</th><th scope="col">US 10-year (%)</th><th scope="col">CAD per USD</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2026-10-01</td><td>3.62</td><td>5.24</td><td>1.4243</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-02</td><td>3.60</td><td>5.28</td><td>1.4246</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-05</td><td>3.63</td><td>5.31</td><td>1.4254</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-06</td><td>3.59</td><td>5.27</td><td>1.4226</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-07</td><td>3.60</td><td>5.28</td><td>1.4257</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-08</td><td>3.60</td><td>5.22</td><td>1.4240</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-09</td><td>3.54</td><td>5.24</td><td>1.4271</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-13</td><td>3.55</td><td>5.21</td><td>1.4222</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-14</td><td>3.55</td><td>5.19</td><td>1.4263</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-15</td><td>3.53</td><td>5.10</td><td>1.4196</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-16</td><td>3.54</td><td>5.09</td><td>1.4154</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-19</td><td>3.64</td><td>5.15</td><td>1.4097</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-20</td><td>3.62</td><td>5.12</td><td>1.4054</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-21</td><td>3.62</td><td>5.13</td><td>1.4057</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-22</td><td>3.50</td><td>4.96</td><td>1.4021</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-23</td><td>3.56</td><td>5.00</td><td>1.3993</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-26</td><td>3.58</td><td>5.00</td><td>1.4025</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-27</td><td>3.57</td><td>4.99</td><td>1.4038</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-28</td><td>3.62</td><td>5.05</td><td>1.4036</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-29</td><td>3.58</td><td>4.98</td><td>1.3985</td></tr><tr><td>2026-10-30</td><td>3.51</td><td>4.94</td><td>1.3960</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Canada yield is the Bank of Canada five-year benchmark. The US series is the Treasury ten-year constant-maturity par yield. The chart shows basis-point changes from October 1. The October 12 holiday has no observation. USD/CAD is an indicative daily average.</p><h3>Monthly inflation measures</h3><div class="mia-tbl-w"><table class="mia-tbl"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Headline CPI y/y (%)</th><th scope="col">CPI ex gasoline (%)</th><th scope="col">Gap (pp)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>1.9</td><td>1.7</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>2.6</td><td>2.6</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>2.3</td><td>2.5</td><td>-0.2</td></tr><tr><td>2025-04</td><td>1.7</td><td>2.6</td><td>-0.9</td></tr><tr><td>2025-05</td><td>1.7</td><td>2.5</td><td>-0.8</td></tr><tr><td>2025-06</td><td>1.9</td><td>2.5</td><td>-0.6</td></tr><tr><td>2025-07</td><td>1.7</td><td>2.5</td><td>-0.8</td></tr><tr><td>2025-08</td><td>1.9</td><td>2.4</td><td>-0.5</td></tr><tr><td>2025-09</td><td>2.4</td><td>2.6</td><td>-0.2</td></tr><tr><td>2025-10</td><td>2.2</td><td>2.6</td><td>-0.4</td></tr><tr><td>2025-11</td><td>2.2</td><td>2.6</td><td>-0.4</td></tr><tr><td>2025-12</td><td>2.4</td><td>3.0</td><td>-0.6</td></tr><tr><td>2026-01</td><td>2.3</td><td>3.0</td><td>-0.7</td></tr><tr><td>2026-02</td><td>1.8</td><td>2.4</td><td>-0.6</td></tr><tr><td>2026-03</td><td>2.4</td><td>2.2</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>2026-04</td><td>2.8</td><td>2.0</td><td>0.8</td></tr><tr><td>2026-05</td><td>3.2</td><td>2.2</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>2026-06</td><td>2.8</td><td>2.2</td><td>0.6</td></tr><tr><td>2026-07</td><td>3.0</td><td>2.2</td><td>0.8</td></tr><tr><td>2026-08</td><td>3.0</td><td>2.4</td><td>0.6</td></tr><tr><td>2026-09</td><td>3.1</td><td>2.2</td><td>0.9</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The CPI series is the Statistics Canada October 19 vintage. September GDP is a separate preliminary estimate. December and January Brent are different contracts. Hormuz traffic estimates that use different definitions are excluded from a combined series.</p><h3>HDQ archive trail</h3><p>Coverage: 106 English desk articles, including the standalone Thanksgiving economy note, 21 Daily Threads and five Weekend Editions. The archive links below identify representative articles for each pattern.</p><ol><li><strong>From the Strait of Hormuz to the gas pump</strong> · <a href="https://hdq.ca/2026/10/13/physical-brent-45-dollars-above-futures-hormuz-shut-wcs-discount-widest-since-2023" title="Physical Brent Trades $45 Above Futures as Hormuz Stays Shut, and Canadian Heavy Crude Sits at the Wrong End of the Spread">Oct 13 · Geo</a>, <a href="https://hdq.ca/2026/10/30/headline-inflation-3-1-percent-core-near-2-bank-of-canada-hikes-into-the-gap" title="Headline Inflation Is 3.1%, Core Is Near 2%, and the Bank of Canada Just Hiked Into That Gap">Oct 30 · Economy</a>, <a href="ht